alias ExitStatus = std::process::ExitStatus;
alias Inputs = std::process::Inputs;

public fn run(inputs: Inputs) -> status: ExitStatus pure waits doc "The html_tokenizer_oracle entry, invoked as `html_tokenizer_oracle PATH` with PATH naming the case file that tests/html/oracle.py writes, relative to the working directory: per case, a header line `STATE LAST_START_TAG_LENGTH INPUT_LENGTH` in decimal with STATE indexing Data, RCDATA, RAWTEXT, script data, PLAINTEXT and CDATA section in that order, the last start tag's bytes, the input's bytes and a line feed. For every case it creates a tokenizer in that state with that last start tag, calls next_token over the input until EndOfFile and prints one line, the token list as JSON in html5lib-tests' representation: [\"DOCTYPE\", name, public id, system id, correctness] with null for an absent part and correctness false exactly when force_quirks is set, [\"StartTag\", name, attributes] with the attributes as a JSON object in the given order and a fourth element true only when self_closing, [\"EndTag\", name], [\"Comment\", data], and one [\"Character\", data] per run of consecutive Characters tokens, in which an empty Characters token contributes nothing; EndOfFile prints nothing. Strings are the token text's WTF-8 bytes with the quotation mark and reverse solidus escaped and each byte below 0x20 as a \\u00XX escape. A case prints a JSON string naming the fault and the zero-based index of the token instead when a span or attribute range lies outside the token text, when a StartTag repeats an attribute name, or when no EndOfFile arrives within 4 times the input length plus 64 tokens; it names the fault alone when the last start tag is longer than tokenizer_new admits. It exits with 0 after printing every case, and with 2, after the cases before it, when the case file cannot be read or is malformed.";
